8.6 Practice: the least punctual plane
These steps follow the in-class exercise. Every plane has a tail number, in the tailnum column. Which plane had the worst on-time record, meaning it left late the most times?
Step 1: set up. Start a new R Markdown file, and load the packages and look at the data:
library(tidyverse)
library(nycflights13)

glimpse(flights)
Step 2: decide what “late” means. This is a choice, not a fact in the data. Is a flight late if it leaves one minute after its scheduled time (dep_delay &gt; 0)? Or if it arrives more than 15 minutes late (arr_delay &gt; 15)? The US Department of Transportation counts a flight as late if it arrives 15 minutes or more after its scheduled time (arr_delay &gt;= 15). Pick one and write it down.
Step 3: count the late flights for each plane. Keep the late flights with filter(), group them by plane, count each group with n(), and sort. Try it first:

Please enable JavaScript to experience the dynamic code cell content on this page.


Show the answer to step 3

flights |&gt;
  filter(dep_delay &gt; 0) |&gt;
  group_by(tailnum) |&gt;
  summarize(n_late = n()) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(n_late))
N258JB, a JetBlue plane, left late 186 times in 2013. If you define late as arr_delay &gt; 15 instead, N258JB still comes first, with 121 late arrivals. But with the Department of Transportation’s rule, arr_delay &gt;= 15, a different plane wins: N725MQ, with 126 late arrivals to N258JB’s 123. Changing &gt; to &gt;= changed the answer, which is why step 2 matters.

Step 4: be fair to busy planes. N258JB flew 427 times in 2013, so of course it was late a lot: it flew a lot. A fairer measure is the share of each plane’s flights that left late. Count all the flights and the late ones for each plane, then divide:
flights |&gt;
  group_by(tailnum) |&gt;
  summarize(
    n_flights = n(),
    n_late = sum(dep_delay &gt; 0, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(share_late = n_late / n_flights) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(share_late))
sum() of a logical vector counts the TRUEs, because R treats TRUE as 1 and FALSE as 0. Look at the top of the result. Planes that flew once or twice, and were late once, have a share of 1, which tells you nothing. Add filter(n_flights &gt;= 100) before the arrange() to keep the planes with a decent record. Also look for a row where tailnum is NA: 2,512 flights, all of them cancelled, have no tail number, and group_by() puts them together in one group. drop_na(tailnum) removes it.

